{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794391105,"nonce":"SmAMzFQPq894TdmDgzS3LtfU3rE=","title":"GPU animation P1: animation descriptors and closed-form curves in blinc_animation","message":"Part of the GPU-deferral plan (umbrella issue). Pure CPU work, additive API.\n\nAn animation becomes plain data: `AnimationDescriptor { start_ms, duration_ms, from, to, curve, initial_velocity }`, evaluated against the frame clock. No closures, so it is also what can cross the ABI (see 36603e0, one animation engine for the DSL, blinc_ts and ashui).\n\n**Curves.** `Curve::{Linear, CubicBezier(x1,y1,x2,y2), Steps(n, jump), Spring{stiffness, damping, mass}}`. Springs use the closed-form damped solution in its three regimes (under, critical, over damped), so the value and velocity at any time `t` are direct, with no stepping. Today's `Spring` is integrated with RK4 in substeps of at most 1/120 s and settles on absolute thresholds (|x - target| \u003c 0.01, |v| \u003c 0.1) that ignore units.\n\n**Functions on the descriptor.** `evaluate(now) -\u003e f32`, `velocity(now) -\u003e f32`, `is_finished(now)`, `retarget(now, new_to) -\u003e AnimationDescriptor`. Retargeting a spring keeps value and velocity (what `Spring::set_target` does today). Retargeting a CSS transition keeps today's behaviour: a new lane from the current value, full duration restarted, velocity not carried. Colour channels use the premultiplied sRGB interpolation the CSS lerp uses today (`lerp_opt_color`); a shared helper keeps CPU and shader the same.\n\n**The CPU mirror is the source of truth for read-back.** `AnimatedValue::get`, `Spring::value`, `AnimatedTimeline::get`, `MotionBindings::get_*` and `MotionHandle::progress` evaluate the descriptor on demand instead of reading state a per-frame tick wrote. Nothing has to tick for them to be right. Where a signal or effect is subscribed to the value, the scheduler still wakes each frame, exactly as now.\n\n**One definition of the maths.** Write the curve functions once in russl-compatible Rust and keep a Rust mirror; both are tested against one file of shared vectors. If russl later grows a CPU target the mirror can come from the same source, but the plan does not depend on that.\n\n**Time semantics change.** The current integrators cap dt, so a stalled frame pauses an animation. An absolute `start_ms` makes a stalled frame skip ahead, as CSS animations do. This is a visible behaviour change and goes in the CHANGELOG.\n\n**API (blinc_animation, additive):** `Curve`, `AnimationDescriptor`, `Easing::to_curve`, `Spring::to_descriptor(now)`, `AnimatedValue::descriptor()`. Existing types keep working.\n\nAcceptance: CPU evaluation and the shader function agree within 1e-5 on the shared vectors; property tests show retargeting is continuous in value and velocity for springs; no change to the existing animation tests.","files":null}]}